Yes — all of this is done on the Linux machine (the same one that runs your Python UI). Here's the step-by-step.
1. Download the ASI SDK
On the Linux machine, open a terminal:
bashCopycd ~/Downloads
wget https://download.zwoastro.com/software/sdk/ASI_linux_mac_SDK_V1.36.tar.bz2

The filename/version may change. If wget fails, just go to https://www.zwoastro.com/downloads/developers in a browser, grab the "ASI Camera SDK for Linux & Mac", and put it in ~/Downloads/.

2. Extract it
bashCopycd ~/Downloads
tar -xjvf ASI_linux_mac_SDK_*.tar.bz2
cd ASI_linux_mac_SDK_*/
ls
You should see a folder structure like:
Copyinclude/        <- ASICamera2.h (C header, you don't need it)
lib/
  armv6/
  armv7/
  armv8/
  mac/
  x64/          <- for normal Intel/AMD 64-bit PCs
  x86/
README.txt
Check what architecture you have:
bashCopyuname -m

x86_64 → use the x64 folder
aarch64 → use the armv8 folder (e.g. Raspberry Pi 4 64-bit)
armv7l → use the armv7 folder

3. Install the library
Replace x64 below with your architecture folder if different:
bashCopysudo cp lib/x64/libASICamera2.so* /usr/local/lib/
sudo ldconfig
Verify it's found:
bashCopyldconfig -p | grep ASICamera2
You should see something like:
CopylibASICamera2.so.1.36 (libc6,x86-64) => /usr/local/lib/libASICamera2.so.1.36
libASICamera2.so (libc6,x86-64) => /usr/local/lib/libASICamera2.so
If libASICamera2.so (without version suffix) is missing, create the symlink:
bashCopycd /usr/local/lib
sudo ln -sf libASICamera2.so.* libASICamera2.so
sudo ldconfig
4. Install the udev rule (so you don't need sudo)
The SDK ships a rule file, usually at lib/asi.rules:
bashCopycd ~/Downloads/ASI_linux_mac_SDK_*/
sudo cp lib/asi.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
sudo udevadm trigger
This grants your normal user USB access to the camera. Unplug and replug the camera after this step.

Some SDK versions also require your user to be in the plugdev group:
bashCopysudo usermod -aG plugdev $USER
Then log out and back in for that group to take effect.

5. Install the Python bindings
bashCopypip install zwoasi pyqt5 pyserial numpy
If you use a virtual environment, activate it first.
6. Verify everything works
Plug the ASI6200 in via USB, then run this quick check:
bashCopypython3 -c "
import zwoasi as asi
asi.init('/usr/local/lib/libASICamera2.so')
print('Cameras found:', asi.list_cameras())
"
Expected output:
CopyCameras found: ['ZWO ASI6200MM Pro']
If you get:

OSError: libASICamera2.so: cannot open shared object file → step 3 didn't take effect; re-check ldconfig -p | grep ASICamera2
Cameras found: [] with the camera plugged in → udev rule not applied; redo step 4, replug the camera
Permission denied → you're not in plugdev, or the udev rule didn't load; check ls -l /dev/bus/usb/...

7. Run the UI
bashCopypython3 main_ui.py
Open the Camera tab → click Refresh → the ASI6200 should appear in the dropdown → click Connect.

Quick troubleshooting cheatsheet
SymptomFixFileNotFoundError: libASICamera2.so from my driversudo ldconfig didn't run, or wrong arch copiedCamera listed but Connect fails with permission errorudev rule not installed → step 4, replugUI hangs on connectPlug into a USB 3.0 port (blue), not USB 2.0 — the ASI6200 is bandwidth-hungryLive view very slowConfirm USB 3.0 cable + port, use RAW8, set bin to 2 or 4
